Daniel Kraft <d@domob.eu> wrote:
> Patric Mueller wrote:
>>
>> Shouldn't it be possible to start a C++ rewrite of NetHack with the
>> existing code base by changing only part of the code to C++?
>>
>> I haven't done it in practice on a project with that much LOC but
>> Stroustroup justified a lot of the brain dead features of C++ with
>> this argument.
>
> Of course this could be one way to go if we wanted some visible
> results soon, but on the other hand I doubt this will be effective if
> we really want a robust re-design from ground up.

Something that's really hard to do with the current NetHack code base
are unit tests.

I do unit tests in NetHack-De because the parsing code for a German
wish obviously quite different from an English wish. Everything else
is mostly replacing strings and not changing code.

I did get to single out the one function needed. Now I just let the
whole game compile with the tests being started when the game gets a
command line option.

> I did implement some interpreters myself in the past myself for this
> kind of thing, but using Guile in one's project is astonishing easy
> (at least it seemed to me).  But while I like Scheme (the underlying
> language for Guile), I would understand if others prefered a real
> imperative language instead.  I guess there are ready-to-use
> frameworks for JavaScript, Python and other similar languages out
> there, too.

Lua seems to be a popular choice, too.

From a few tutorials I found, embedding Lua into a C program is simple
and calling C functions from Lua is straight forward.

